  "query_preview": "..A weak shortwave trough is currently shifting across\nthe region, bringing an area of showers to portions of central and\nsouthern Utah. CAMS suggest additional development ahead of this\nshortwave will occur across the southern mountains. Given surface\ndewpoints in the 30s to lower 40s and mediocre instability, \nexpect this convection to be mainly outflow dominant, trending\ntoward the adjacent lower desert areas through early evening. Main\nthreat will be gusty, microburst winds in excess of 40 mph and\nbrief heavy rain.\n\nAttention will then turn to increasing potential for widespread\nheavy rain across the region from as early as late Saturday\nthrough at least Tuesday. A strong upper level trough shifting\ninto the Pacific Northwest will combine with now Hurricane Rachel\nto bring deep tropical moisture into Utah, as early as Saturday\nnight. Guidance has shifted quite a bit toward this phased\nsolution, with only 10% of members now favoring a more\nprogressive, drier solution. \n\nHow long the moisture sticks around is another question. Around\n50% of members suggest the trough, along with PW anomalies on the\norder of 98+ percentile remains over the West through at least\nTuesday night. The remaining 50% of members are more progressive,\nclearing the trough sometime Monday. \n\nRegardless, confidence is high with 90% of members showing a\nvariation that phases the strong Pacific trough with tropical\nmoisture associated with Rachel, that Utah will see a period of\nmoderate to heavy rainfall. Current guidance suggests 1 to 2\ninches of rain across much of Utah, with locally in excess of \n2.5-3.00 inches, particularly across southern Utah as the most \nprobable range for the event. With colder temperatures associated\nwith this Pacific trough, some higher elevation locations above\n8500 feet may see light snow accumulations Tuesday. \n\nGiven it is only Tuesday, no products will be issued for this\nenhanced flash flood threat, but those with outdoor plans for the\nholiday weekend, particularly Sunday and Monday, should be\nprepared to make alternate arrangements as many areas of southern\nUtah such as slot canyons, normally dry washes, streams and areas\nnear recent burn scars may see a heightened threat of flash\nflooding. Rises are also likely on all area streams and rivers.",
